    "lab-confirmed": {
      "type": "copper-bottles-cut-open-lab-confirmed",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "I didn’t want this to be my opinion",
        "body": "<p>A photograph of a cut bottle shows you there is a second metal in the wall. It does not tell you which metal, in what proportion, or whether anything else came along with it.</p><p>So one sample from each of the ten bottles went to CALIMP for composition analysis — copper percentage, lead, zinc, cadmium. The same parameters the better sellers in this category publish, so the numbers are comparable.</p><p>For reference, a properly made copper bottle reports around <strong>99.5%</strong> or higher copper, lead roughly ten times under the <strong>FDA</strong> limit, and no detectable cadmium. That is the bar. It is not a hard bar to hit if you are making the bottle out of copper.</p><p>All ten reports are published in full, including the results for the expensive bottle, and including anything in them I would rather not have found.</p>",
        "link_label": "Read all ten lab reports (PDF)",
        "link_url": "",
        "photo_fallback": "copper-bottles-cut-open-lab-reports.jpg"
      }
    },
    "math": {
      "type": "copper-bottles-cut-open-math",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "The math doesn’t leave room",
        "body": "<p>Work backwards from the price tag.</p><p>A bottle listed at $29 on a marketplace leaves the seller somewhere <strong>around $9 to $12</strong> after platform fees, advertising, returns and shipping. <strong>That is the whole budget</strong> — metal, manufacture, cap, packaging, freight from Asia, and margin.</p><p>Now price the honest version. Machine-made copper bottles wholesale out of India at roughly $4 a unit, and that is the product category we are talking about: pressed on a line, sprayed inside, no testing. <strong>A bottle raised</strong> and hammered by hand is about an hour of somebody’s work. Batch testing at a <strong>laboratory</strong> is a real invoice per production run. Leaving the interior bare means accepting returns from customers who think tarnish is a defect, which costs money too.</p><p><strong>None of that fits inside $9.</strong></p><p>So something has to come out, and the order it comes out in is predictable. First the hand work — a die is faster and the listing can still say hammered. Then the testing, because nobody checks. Then the interior gets lacquered, because a bottle that never darkens generates fewer complaints. And if the number still does not work, the copper itself gets thinner, until it is a coating on something cheaper.</p><p><strong>That is not a conspiracy.</strong> Nobody in the chain is lying on purpose at every step. It is just what $29 buys once you subtract the fees, and the listing is written to make the subtraction invisible.</p><p>Which is also why the word copper does so much work in these titles. It is not a regulated claim on drinkware. It can legally describe a colour, a finish, a plating or a solid metal — four different products, one word, and the distinction buried in the specification block where nobody reads.</p>"
      }
    },
