    "tenth-bottle": {
      "type": "copper-bottles-cut-open-tenth-bottle",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "The tenth bottle, and the part where I have to declare something",
        "body": "<p>I bought a tenth bottle as a control — the most expensive one I could find that published a lab report before I paid, rather than after I asked. It cost $59. It is made by a company called Neera.</p><p>Here is the declaration: <strong>Neera paid for this testing,</strong> and this article is published on their site. You should weigh everything above accordingly. The reason I agreed to it is that they asked for exactly one thing — that their bottle go on the same saw as the other nine, and that the <strong>result be published</strong> whatever it was.</p><p><strong>It went through</strong> the same seven steps. Same magnet, same scale, same torch, same jig, same bench, same lab.</p><p>One material, all the way through. No core, no plating boundary, no film on the inside wall. The colour is uniform from the outer surface to the inner because there is nothing in there but copper. The hammer pattern does not repeat, because a person put it there. The lab came back at 99.9%.</p><p>It is the only bottle of the ten that was what it said it was. That is a lower bar than it sounds like, and nine companies failed it.</p>",
        "photo": "shopify://shop_images/c6280025ec04ba054f43897119b997ccc60cea0a_1.jpg",
        "photo_fallback": "copper-bottles-cut-open-tenth-bottle.jpg",
        "caption": "Bottle 10 · $59 · 99.9% copper · no lining, no core"
      }
    },
    "what-30-buys": {
      "type": "copper-bottles-cut-open-what-30-buys",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "What the difference actually pays for",
        "body": "<p>Going back to the arithmetic from earlier, here is the same subtraction run in the other direction.</p><p>An hour of hand-raising and hammering per bottle, which is why no two of them are <strong>identical</strong> and why the dimple pattern does not repeat.</p><p>A <strong>laboratory invoice</strong> against every production batch, not every hundredth one, with the certificate published on the product page rather than produced on request.</p><p>A bare interior, and the returns that come with it from people who <strong>think</strong> a darkening bottle is a <strong>broken bottle.</strong></p><p>A cap made from the same solid copper as the body, which is the single most common place to save $2.</p><p><strong>Stock held</strong> in the United States, so the thing <strong>arrives</strong> in a day or two <strong>instead</strong> of three weeks from overseas.</p><p>And the care brush and the sleeve in the box, rather than sold back to you as accessories.</p><p>Thirty dollars, once. Copper does not degrade, and with a lemon and a minute a week the bottle outlasts the argument.</p>",
        "photo_fallback": "copper-bottles-cut-open-complete-set.jpg"
      }
    },
